{
  "dom_id": "step-q70815",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(0,0,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=5t \\\\ z=1-t\\end{aligned}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,3⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨2,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨2,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,5,-1⟩ & =⟨0,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,5,-1⟩ & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,-1⟩ & =-3 \\\\ 2x+5y-z & =-3 \\\\ 2x+5y-z+3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
